Cross-Validated Feature Selection
ML Project documentation log (continuation of the original Feature Selection log). Notebook: ML_Project_Feature_Selection_CV.ipynb. This version reflects the third run (full pipeline with fold-wise imputation, StopDescription added).
With feature selection done inside each CV fold, no method beats keeping all 20 variables. The consensus rule keeps 13 variables that are stable across folds (16 when run once on the full training set), at a cost of 0.0013 AUC (logistic regression) or 0.0035 (gradient boosting).

What Changed and Why
The original script fitted every selection step (univariate tests, mutual information, lasso, random forest, multivariable regression) on the whole training set and only compared feature sets with cross-validation afterwards. The held-out folds had therefore already influenced which features were chosen, so the CV scores were optimistic. This was the last item in the original log ("Redo feature selection within each fold").
The new notebook puts the selection inside each CV fold:
The preprocessing code from preprocessing_pipeline.ipynb is reused as the front end of one scikit-learn Pipeline: bin rare levels, impute, one-hot encode, scale, select features, final model.
cross_validate runs on that pipeline, so binning, imputation, encoding, scaling and the selection itself are fitted on the training part of each fold only. The validation part of the fold is only transformed.
Selection works on the original variable: all dummies of a categorical (and its missing-value indicator) are kept or dropped together.
The test set is never loaded.
Folds are identical for every method (5-fold stratified, seed 10), so results are paired.

Setup
The row count, positive rate and dropped text columns were printed in the first run (121,317 rows, 5.507% positive, 6,681 events; BlockAddress and StopDate dropped as non-numeric). The printout for the current run was not provided, so these figures are not re-confirmed for the current data file.

Candidate variables: 20.
Categorical (8): VehicleStyle, VehicleColor, SubjectRace, SubjectEyeColor, SubjectHairColor, Zip, StopDescription, District. District is a numeric district code with 8 levels; it is converted to text and one-hot encoded like the other categoricals. StopDescription is new in this run.
Numeric (6): SubjectAge, SubjectHeight, SubjectWeight, VehicleYear, hour, month.
Binary (6): SubjectGender, isLA, SubjectHasPhotoID, night, weekend, isDriver.

isTrafficViolation was a candidate in the earlier runs but is not in this notebook's column lists. The candidate count (20) equals the number of listed variables, so it appears not to be in the current data file (assuming every listed column is present). It also does not appear in either selected list below, so results are not directly comparable with the earlier runs.
Compared with the 24 in the original log, year, Zone, Latitude and Longitude are excluded because the preprocessing pipeline drops them (they are in its drop list). year was the second-strongest single predictor in the original log (OR 0.65 per SD, AUC 0.641) and is not a candidate here, so the AUCs below are not comparable to the original log's 0.82.

Preprocessing: the full pipeline, fitted inside each fold: rare levels under 1% pooled into Other; ordinal encoding of categoricals; iterative imputation of missing values (histogram gradient boosting regressor, up to 5 iterations); imputed values rounded and clipped to the training range; one-hot encoding of categoricals and scaling of numeric columns. The first two runs used the simple pipeline (median / most-frequent / "Missing" imputation with missing indicators), with one fix to the original calls: build_simple_pipeline was being passed dis_cols where it expects bin_cols, which sent age, height, weight, vehicle year, month and hour through the pipeline twice (once scaled, once raw). That fix no longer applies, because the full pipeline is used.

Final models (used only to score each selection): L2 logistic regression, and histogram gradient boosting (learning rate 0.1, 200 iterations, early stopping).

Metrics: ROC AUC (main), average precision, Brier score.

Selection Methods (all run inside each fold)
Method
Rule for keeping a variable
Why it is uniquely useful
Pros
Cons
none
Baseline, keep everything
The reference point: shows whether any selection is worth doing at all (here, none improved AUC).
No selection variance; best or tied-best AUC.
No reduction and no interpretability gain.
lasso
L1 logistic regression on standardized features; C chosen by inner 3-fold CV on AUC; keep if any coefficient is non-zero
Multivariable: judges each variable given all the others at once, so it can drop a variable that adds nothing once the rest are in.
One fit gives a sparse model; handles many dummies; penalty tuned on AUC.
At the best-AUC C it keeps almost everything (19.6 of 20); linear and additive only; picks arbitrarily among correlated variables; one non-zero dummy keeps the whole variable.
lasso_1se
Same, but the strongest penalty whose inner CV AUC is within 1 SE of the best
The only lasso variant that actually prunes: it prefers the simplest model that is statistically as good as the best.
Sparser than lasso (17.8 of 20) with almost no AUC loss; principled stopping rule.
Still prunes little here; the SE comes from only 3 inner folds, so the kept set varies by fold (SD 0.7 variables).
rf_impurity
Random forest impurity importance (summed over dummies) must beat a random noise column
Captures non-linear effects and interactions at almost no cost: importance is a by-product of one forest fit.
Fast; non-linear; has a built-in noise yardstick.
Computed on training rows; biased toward continuous and many-level variables. Kept about 6 variables and lost about 0.030 to 0.035 AUC. See the comparison with permutation importance below.
rf_perm
Random forest permutation importance on an inner 25% hold-out; all columns of a variable shuffled together; keep if the AUC drop is above 2 SD and above 0 (5 repeats, up to 8,000 rows)
Measures how much held-out predictive accuracy depends on the variable, non-linearly, with a categorical judged as a whole.
Uses rows the forest did not train on; result is in AUC points; less biased toward many-level variables than impurity.
Correlated variables mask each other (for example Zip and District); the 2 SD rule is lenient and noisy with 5 repeats; forest trained on only 75% of the fold; slower.
mutual_info
MI must beat the largest MI from a shuffled target (30,000-row subsample)
Model-free: detects any dependence, including non-monotonic patterns a linear test cannot see, with a data-based chance threshold.
No model assumptions; explicit noise floor.
Looks at one variable at a time (ignores redundancy and interactions); hard to estimate for sparse dummies and a 5.5% positive rate; the subsample makes it noisy (SD 2.2 variables). Kept about 10 variables and lost about 0.008 to 0.013 AUC.
univariate_lrt
Likelihood-ratio test of the variable alone vs. intercept-only, Benjamini–Hochberg adjusted, alpha 0.05
The only formal significance test: each variable gets one test across all its dummies, with multiple-testing control.
Whole-variable test with a stated error rate; fast and deterministic; easy to explain.
Ignores other variables, so a proxy can look significant and a variable that matters only after adjustment is missed (hour: 0 of 5 folds in the second run). With this many rows nearly everything is significant (18 of 20).
consensus
Keep if at least 3 of 5 votes: univariate_lrt, mutual_info, lasso, rf_impurity, rf_perm
Combines checks with different assumptions (univariate vs. multivariable, linear vs. non-linear, model-based vs. model-free), so no single method's bias decides alone.
Prunes about 5 variables (20 to 14.8) for a small AUC cost (-0.0013 logistic regression, -0.0035 gradient boosting); a variable backed by several methods is less likely to be a chance pick.
The 3-vote threshold is arbitrary; the lenient voters (univariate_lrt, lasso) add votes, so rf_perm, rf_impurity and mutual_info usually decide; slowest, since it runs all five.

The original log also used a multivariable logistic regression drop-one test as a vote. It is not part of the new consensus; the lasso covers that role.

Random forest: impurity vs. permutation importance
Impurity importance shows what the forest used to build itself; permutation importance shows what its predictions on new rows depend on. Both come from the same forest (150 trees, trained on 75% of the training fold, with a random noise column added), but they answer different questions and so support different decisions.
Aspect
Impurity importance (rf_impurity)
Permutation importance (rf_perm)
Question answered
How much did splits on this variable reduce node impurity while the forest was built?
How much does held-out AUC fall when this variable's values are shuffled?
Data used
Training rows the forest was fitted on
Inner 25% hold-out rows the forest never saw
Cost
Free by-product of fitting
Extra predictions per variable and repeat (5 repeats, up to 8,000 rows)
Categoricals
Summed over a variable's dummies
All of a variable's dummies shuffled together
Known bias
Favours continuous and many-level variables, which offer more split points; a pure-noise column can outscore real variables (it ranked 7th in the original log)
Less biased toward many-level variables, but shares credit unevenly between correlated variables
Correlated variables
Credit is split between them across trees
Shuffling one leaves its partner intact, so the drop can be near zero even if both matter (for example Zip and District)
Threshold used here
Must beat one random noise column
AUC drop above 2 SD across repeats and above 0
Scale
Relative share of total importance
AUC points lost


How this changes the interpretation for feature selection
Impurity is one-sided evidence. A variable clearly above the noise column is probably worth keeping. A variable below it is not shown to be useless, because the measure discounts binary and categorical variables.
Permutation is the better basis for keeping or dropping, since it is measured on rows the forest did not fit. A clear drop supports keeping the variable. A near-zero drop is weaker evidence for dropping it when a correlated substitute exists, so refit without the variable before removing one of a pair such as Zip and District.
The results in this project show the gap. rf_impurity kept about 6 variables (6.2 per fold) and lost 0.030 to 0.035 AUC. rf_perm kept about 15 variables (15.4 per fold) and lost 0.0014 to 0.0016. In the second run, District, Zip and SubjectRace were selected in 0 of 5 folds by impurity and in 5 of 5 by permutation; per-method fold counts for the latest run are not available.
Practical rule: use impurity to rank and screen, use permutation to decide what to keep, and never drop a variable on impurity alone. In consensus each is one vote among five.
Both depend on one forest and one inner split per fold, so expect some variation between folds (rf_perm kept 15.4 ± 1.5 variables).

