Reading it:
No method beats keeping everything. Change in AUC against none:
Logistic regression: lasso -0.0000, lasso_1se -0.0004, univariate_lrt -0.0004, consensus -0.0013, rf_perm -0.0016, mutual_info -0.0081, rf_impurity -0.0346.
Gradient boosting: lasso -0.0008, rf_perm -0.0014, lasso_1se -0.0028, consensus -0.0035, univariate_lrt -0.0058, mutual_info -0.0126, rf_impurity -0.0303.
The first five or six methods are small next to the fold-to-fold SD of about 0.006–0.008. The notebook also printed paired differences against none; they were not included in the output I was given, so this judgement is from the means.
rf_impurity costs 0.030 to 0.035 AUC and mutual_info 0.008 to 0.013. They keep far fewer variables (6.2 and 9.6) than the other methods, and the AUC falls accordingly, which suggests the variables they drop carry signal. mutual_info is also the noisiest method (AUC SD 0.012–0.014; SD 2.2 variables kept). Impurity importance is already known to favour continuous variables (see the comparison above). Neither should be used on its own.
lasso, lasso_1se and univariate_lrt barely prune (17.8–19.6 of 20 variables). With this many rows almost everything is significant or gets a non-zero coefficient, as the original log also warned.
consensus removes about 5 variables (20 → 14.8 per fold) for a small AUC cost, similar to the second run (-0.0018 and -0.0039). Gradient boosting average precision falls more than AUC does (0.2836 to 0.2755; see below).
Gradient boosting beats logistic regression by about 0.0193 AUC with all variables (0.8255 vs 0.8062), and by 0.014 to 0.024 under every selector. The original log concluded that "logistic regression is adequate" from a gap of about 0.01, so non-linear effects or interactions may matter more than that log suggested.

