The stable list is a subset of the full-train list. The three extra variables (VehicleStyle, SubjectHairColor, month) were chosen on the full training set but in fewer than 4 of 5 folds, so they are borderline. Compared with the second run, StopDescription is new, and isTrafficViolation and isLA, which were in both lists before, do not appear in either list.

Conclusions
Feature selection does not improve accuracy here. Keeping all 20 variables gives the best or tied-best AUC for both models (0.8062 logistic regression, 0.8255 gradient boosting). The case for selecting is a simpler model, not a better score.
Recommended reduced set: the 13 stable consensus variables (StopDescription, SubjectAge, SubjectGender, VehicleYear, SubjectWeight, SubjectRace, Zip, night, hour, SubjectHeight, isDriver, SubjectHasPhotoID, District). The per-fold consensus rule costs about 0.0013 AUC (logistic regression) to 0.0035 (gradient boosting) relative to using everything; the fixed 13-variable list itself was not scored separately. If accuracy is the only goal, keep all 20.
Use gradient boosting if the goal is the highest score; it leads logistic regression by about 0.0193 AUC with all variables.

Glossary (How to Read Each Output)
Variables kept: number of original variables (not dummy columns) the selector kept in a fold, averaged over the 5 folds, with the SD across folds.
ROC AUC (outer fold): ability to rank searched stops above non-searched ones (0.5 = coin flip, 1 = perfect), measured on a fold that played no part in the selection or fitting.
Average precision: area under the precision–recall curve; more informative than AUC when only 5.5% of stops lead to a search (a random model would score about 0.055).
Brier score: mean squared error of the predicted probabilities; lower is better.
Selection frequency: the number of outer folds (out of 5) in which a variable was kept. Variables chosen in only 1–2 folds are not reliably useful.
lasso vs lasso_1se: the best-AUC penalty keeps almost everything; the 1-SE version picks the strongest penalty that is statistically as good, so it prunes more.
rf_perm: drop in held-out AUC when a variable's values are shuffled; a variable's dummies are shuffled together so a categorical is judged as a whole.
consensus: a variable is kept if at least 3 of the 5 voting methods choose it.
Stable (final list): selected in at least 80% of the outer folds.
Random intercept / ICC (random-effects extension): a random intercept gives each district its own baseline search rate, shrunk toward the overall average. The ICC is the share of the variation in the log-odds of a search that lies between districts; near zero means the district effect adds little.


